From: SF2000 Linux port
Subject: mips: sf2000: load extra syscall arguments from the NOMMU stack

The early SF2000 KSEG-stack workaround bypassed the normal uaccess loads for
arguments five through eight, but replaced all four arguments with zero.  That
was enough for the initial four-argument file I/O probes, while silently
breaking normal interfaces such as mmap(addr, len, prot, flags, fd, offset):
every mmap used fd 0 and returned ENODEV before reaching the target driver.

The bFLT stack lives in ordinary RAM through its KSEG0 alias and syscall entry
is already executing in kernel mode, so load these words directly.  KSEG0 is
the validated SF2000 no-MMU execution model and does not need an exception-
protected user access instruction.  This restores the complete o32 syscall
calling convention for no-MMU programs.

--- a/arch/mips/kernel/scall32-o32.S
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/scall32-o32.S
@@ -136,10 +136,10 @@ loads_done:
 	b	sf2000_stack_args_ready
 	nop
 sf2000_kseg_stack_args:
-	move	t5, zero
-	move	t6, zero
-	move	t7, zero
-	move	t8, zero
+	lw	t5, 16(t0)		# argument #5 from direct KSEG0 stack
+	lw	t6, 20(t0)		# argument #6 from direct KSEG0 stack
+	lw	t7, 24(t0)		# argument #7 from direct KSEG0 stack
+	lw	t8, 28(t0)		# argument #8 from direct KSEG0 stack
 sf2000_stack_args_ready:
 #endif
 
